.docname {Rate Limit}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Crate | A Rust library other Rust code builds against | `rate-limit` |
    | Middleware | Code that runs before a route handler and can stop the request | `rate_limit` |
    | Window | The stretch of time a count covers before it starts over | 60 s |
    | `x-forwarded-for` | A header listing the addresses a request passed through, oldest first | `203.0.113.5, 198.51.100.7` |
    | Peer address | The address of whoever opened the TCP connection | the edge proxy |
    | `429` | HTTP status "Too Many Requests" | the answer once a client is over the limit |

Rust crate at `backend/libs/rate-limit`. It caps how often one client may call a service: **300 requests per 60 s per client IP**, then `429`.

| Used by | Wired in |
|---|---|
| `chat` | `src/lib.rs` `build_router`, state in `src/state.rs` |
| `digital-twin` | same |
| `notification` | same |

Dependencies: `axum` (the `tokio` feature, for `ConnectInfo`) and `dashmap`.

---

## What a service gets

| Item | What it is |
|---|---|
| `RateLimiter::new(enabled)` | The counters. `Clone` shares them, so every route counts into one map |
| `rate_limit` | The middleware, used with `axum::middleware::from_fn_with_state(limiter, rate_limit)` |
| `enabled: false` | Lets every request through. Tests use it, because they send more than 300 requests |

The service must serve with `into_make_service_with_connect_info::<SocketAddr>()`. Without it there is no peer address to fall back on.

---

## How one request is counted

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        R["request"] --> E{"enabled?"}
        E -- no --> N["next handler"]
        E -- yes --> I["client IP: last x-forwarded-for entry, else peer, else 0.0.0.0"]
        I --> S["sweep idle clients, at most once per window"]
        S --> W{"window for this IP older than 60 s?"}
        W -- yes --> Z["start over at 0"]
        W -- no --> C["count + 1"]
        Z --> C
        C --> L{"count over 300?"}
        L -- yes --> T["429"]
        L -- no --> N

Example, one client behind the edge:

| Step | Request from | `x-forwarded-for` | Counted as | Count | Answer |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Ada | `198.51.100.7` | `198.51.100.7` | 1 | `200` |
| 2–300 | Ada | same | same | 300 | `200` |
| 301 | Ada | same | same | 301 | `429` |
| 302 | Ada, forging a header | `10.0.0.1, 198.51.100.7` | `198.51.100.7` | 302 | `429` |
| 303 | Bob | `198.51.100.8` | `198.51.100.8` | 1 | `200` |

Step 302 is why the **rightmost** entry counts: the edge appends the address it saw, so only the last entry is one the client cannot write.

---

## Tests

| Test | Checks |
|---|---|
| `client_ip` tests | The edge's entry wins; no header falls back to the peer; no source gives `0.0.0.0`; a malformed header falls back to the peer |
| Middleware tests | 300 pass; the 301st is `429`; a disabled limiter never refuses; one noisy client does not slow another; a new forged address per request does not reset the count; an idle client is forgotten; the count starts over after the window |

```bash
mise exec -- cargo test --manifest-path backend/libs/rate-limit/Cargo.toml
```

---

## Building a service that uses it

The dependency is a path, `../libs/rate-limit`, so each consumer image builds from the repo root. Each `Dockerfile` copies the crate before `cargo chef prepare` **and** before each `cargo chef cook`; a path missing during `cook` fails the build half way. `.github/services.json` lists the folder in each consumer's `depends_on`, so a change here reruns all three.

---

## Gaps

- **The counts live in memory.** Two copies of one service each allow the full 300 per minute.
- **`/health` and `/metrics` count too.** They pass through the same limiter.
- **Many people behind one address share one count.** An office behind one NAT gets 300 per minute in total.
